<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Gotha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1601">1601</a>–<a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ernest the Pious</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest I, called the Pious, founded the Duchy of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> in 1640. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> he rebuilt his lands as a model Lutheran state, introducing compulsory schooling and orderly administration from Friedenstein Castle in Gotha. In 1680 his seven sons divided his inheritance, creating the duchies of Gotha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romhild">Römhild</a>, Eisenberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha.jpg" alt="Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Gotha" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernest_I,_duke_of_Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest was born on 25 December 1601, a surviving son of Johann II, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, and Dorothea Maria of Anhalt. His father died in 1605 and his mother in 1617, and he was brought up strictly: gifted and precocious but not physically strong, and early marked by the piety of his time. A devout Lutheran, he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1631 and fought at Lech, Nördlingen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635 he withdrew from warfare to concentrate on administration and recovery. In 1640, under the partition treaty with his brothers, he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>.</p><p>On 24 October 1636 he married his younger cousin Elisabeth Sophie of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, and when the last duke of that line died childless in 1672 the two duchies were united.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Thirty Years' War Ernest freed his land from debt by careful economy, reduced taxation and left a considerable sum in the treasury at his death. He gave much attention to public security and to an efficient judiciary, and other states took up his regulations as models. He banned torture and dueling, and was a foe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>, though he supported witchcraft trials. His laws forbade secret betrothals and tried to regulate dress, yet promoted agriculture, commerce, learning and art. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedenstein-palace">Friedenstein Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, whose collections began with him, and founded a library that became one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>With Veit Ludwig von Seckendorf and Andreas Reyher he issued the Schulmethodus of 1642, which called for compulsory, graded schooling. It was said that no one in his land could not read and write, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> gymnasium drew pupils from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He also increased the funds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a>. His religious zeal won him the nickname Praying Ernest. He interceded with the emperor for his Austrian co-religionists, supported the Lutheran church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and sent an embassy to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> into Abyssinia, which failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ernest died on 26 March 1675 and was buried in the crypt of St. Margarethenkirche on the Neumarkt, the first of the House of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> to lie there. He and Elisabeth Sophie had 18 children, of whom nine reached adulthood. Frederick I inherited Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg, and the other sons became dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romhild">Römhild</a>, Eisenberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>. He died with the name of father and saviour of his people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> is said to have counted him among the most sagacious of princes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest I of Saxe-Gotha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Ernest, seventh son of <b>Ernest the Pious</b>, received Saalfeld in 1680.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1680 division of the inheritance of <b>Ernest the Pious</b>, his fifth son Christian received Eisenberg and its district, between Gera and Jena, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ernest the Pious</b> introduced compulsory schooling, a famous school method and detailed church and state ordinances, rebuilding a land ruined by the Thirty Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>Ernest, the sixth son of <b>Ernest the Pious</b>, received Hildburghausen in the 1680 partition of the Saxe-Gotha inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen</a></li><li><blockquote>Bernhard I, third son of <b>Ernest the Pious</b>, received Meiningen in the 1680 partition of the Saxe-Gotha inheritance and made the small town his residence, building Elisabethenburg Palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-meiningen">Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ernest the Pious</b> of Saxe-Gotha died in 1675, and his sons ruled jointly until they divided the lands in 1680.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-romhild">Duchy of Saxe-Römhild</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.99,12.43&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Altenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4833 396 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Gotha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5283 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5283 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.99,12.43&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
